\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Top Workflow (Bent-Pipe Architecture): Data is acquired and minimally preprocessed on the satellite before being transmitted to the ground station, where in-depth analysis for anomalies is conducted. Bottom Workflow: The satellite extracts parameters and detects anomalies onboard, transmitting only the relevant anomaly data to the ground station, reducing data transmission and enabling faster detection. From Razzano et al.~\cite {razzano2024ai}. }}{16}{figure.caption.7}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Four representative triplets, each row showing the same scene as observed by real $\Phi $-sat-2, the physics-simulated $\Phi $-sat-2 rendering, and Sentinel-2B (each channel independently contrast-stretched for display). The real acquisitions shows blurier scenes despite the higher resolution and a noisy texture compared to the simulated or source Sentinel-2 view. }}{23}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Band correspondence between Sentinel-2B and $\Phi $-sat-2, from Longépé et al.~\cite {longepe2024orbitalai}. (a) Normalized spectral response functions, Sentinel-2B above and $\Phi $-sat-2 below. (b) Per-band spatial resolution. The correspondence in (a) makes Sentinel-2 usable as the paired source; the residual differences in both panels are the sensor gap this thesis aims to measure. }}{24}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces A representative triplet, each row showing the same scene as observed by real $\Phi $-sat-2, the physics-simulated $\Phi $-sat-2 rendering, and Sentinel-2B (each channel independently contrast-stretched for display). The real acquisitions shows heavy band misalignment artifacts compared to the simulated or source Sentinel-2 view. }}{25}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Left: distribution of the absolute time lag between matched $\Phi $-sat-2 and Sentinel-2 acquisitions (median 6.8 days, maximum 14.9 days, $n=253{,}228$). Right: monthly acquisition volume, Aug. \ 2024--Apr.\ 2026, showing the collection ramp-up in the first eight months.}}{25}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Left: spatial density of $\Phi $-sat-2 products (hexbin, log-scaled patch count per cell). Right: distribution of patches by K\"oppen--Geiger climate group\cite {beck2018present}. }}{26}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.6}{\ignorespaces Left: share of all labelled pixels by \acs {ESA} WorldCover class. Right: number of patches in which each class is the dominant (plurality) class. }}{26}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.7}{\ignorespaces Left: class prior is stable across the train/validation/test split. Right: every one of the 1,287 products contributing to the test set also contributes patches to the training set as the split is patch-level. }}{27}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.8}{\ignorespaces Per-band raw digital-number density by sensor (log-scaled $x$-axis; 400{,}000 sampled pixels). Sentinel-2B (orange) and simulated $\Phi $-sat-2 (green) overlap. Real $\Phi $-sat-2 (blue) is concentrated in lower values, with a smaller secondary lobe extending into the Sentinel-2B/simulated value range in every band. }}{28}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.9}{\ignorespaces The same per-band densities after per-sensor $z$-score normalization of $\sqrt {\text {\acs {DN}}}$. }}{28}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.10}{\ignorespaces Median per-band digital number, normalized by each sensor's own overall band mean (1.0 = that sensor's average). Sentinel-2B and simulated $\Phi $-sat-2 overlap almost exactly; real $\Phi $-sat-2 has a markedly different spectral shape, peaking at Red and dipping at RE1 and \acs {NIR}. }}{29}{figure.caption.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.11}{\ignorespaces Mean cross-sensor approximation error (Equation~\eqref {eq:approx-error}) by dominant \acs {ESA} WorldCover class, over 5,000 patches. Error bars are one standard deviation; the percentage above each bar is that class's share of labelled pixels in the sample. Three classes show >1\% presence in the sample (moss and lichen, snow and ice, mangroves) because they are rare in the dataset, they are still reported for completeness. }}{30}{figure.caption.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.12}{\ignorespaces Patch showing coastal wetland (mean approximation error $1.24$, above the dataset-wide mean of $0.91$). Errors are located over open water, consistent with the class-level pattern of Figure~\ref {fig:approx-error-class}. }}{30}{figure.caption.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.13}{\ignorespaces Patch showing a grassland cutting a forest. This simple scene has a low mean approximation error ($0.96$).}}{31}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.14}{\ignorespaces Patch showing cropland (mean approximation error $0.90$). This real data shows the band misalignment that Equation~\eqref {eq:misalign-l1a} models synthetically. The scalar mean approximation error for this patch ($0.90$) is close to the dataset-wide mean despite the heavy artifact: Equation~\eqref {eq:approx-error} averages absolute per-band intensity differences and is insensitive to spatial/geometric coherence, so it under-represents local misalignment, which is a limitation of the metric. }}{31}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.15}{\ignorespaces The updated degradation pipeline. A source Sentinel-2 L1 product passes through five physical stages in radiance space before conversion back to reflectance, each driven by its own auxiliary input: the centre-wavelength list and spectral response functions for the spectral adjustment, the 4.75\,m target ground sampling distance for the resampling, a misalignment standard deviation for the band-to-band shift, the per-band effective \acs {SNR} for the noise term, and the Nyquist \acs {MTF} for the optical blur. Solar zenith angle, Earth--Sun distance, and solar irradiance are retrieved per scene from the Copernicus Data Space OData \acs {API}. Because every stage is parameterised separately, individual effects can be varied or switched off independently, and the segmentation mask is carried through the pipeline so that labels stay aligned with the degraded imagery. }}{33}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.16}{\ignorespaces Batch normalization compared with domain-specific batch normalization, from Chang et al.~\cite {chang2019dsbn}. (a) A single BatchNorm layer processes source ($x_S$) and target ($x_T$) examples alike, so its statistics describe a mixture of the two domains. (b) A \acs {DSBN} layer carries one branch per domain, and each input is routed to the branch corresponding to its own domain; the other branch, shown greyed, is inactive for that example. All parameters outside the \acs {DSBN} layers remain shared across domains. In this thesis the source domain is Sentinel-2 and the target domain is $\Phi $-sat-2, and the domain of every input is known both during training and on board. }}{38}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.17}{\ignorespaces Cross-architecture distillation with domain-specific normalization. The frozen TerraMind teacher is evaluated only on the Sentinel-2 view, where it is trustworthy; its soft predictions supervise both student branches, which is possible because the two sensor views depict the same scene. The student is forwarded twice per patch, once per sensor, selecting its own batch-normalization statistics each time while sharing all convolutional weights. Both branches also receive the task loss against the single shared WorldCover label map. The contrastive terms were implemented and evaluated but carry zero weight in every result reported in Chapter~\ref {chap:results-discussion}. }}{40}{figure.caption.35}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces \acs {UMAP} projection of pooled embeddings for $800$ co-registered test patches, teacher (left) and distilled student (right), each projected independently. Grey lines join the two sensor views of the same patch. In the teacher's space the paired views are far apart (median link $6.15$ units); in the student's they are effectively coincident ($0.40$ units). }}{44}{figure.caption.39}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Per-layer cosine similarity between raw paired Sentinel-2B and real-$\Phi $-sat-2 patch embeddings from the TerraMind Tiny encoder, averaged over $800$ samples (shaded band: one standard deviation). }}{45}{figure.caption.40}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces A degradation ladder sample from the Sen1Floods benchmark (scene Nigeria 81933, $256\times 256$\,px at $4.75$\,m resolution). Each row varies one factor in isolation across the four severities of Table~\ref {tab:ladder-definition}, from undegraded (left) to full severity L4 (right), while every other stage of the simulator is held fixed. A single contrast stretch, fixed on the undegraded scene, is applied to every panel. }}{46}{figure.caption.42}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Distribution of the domain probe's out-of-fold decision score, the quantity from which \acs {PAD} is computed. Separated modes imply a probe that never errs (\acs {PAD} $\rightarrow 2$); overlapping modes imply chance (\acs {PAD} $\rightarrow 0$). After standardising each domain on its own statistics (right column) the separability vanishes for both models, showing that all of it was per-channel mean and scale, not representational structure. }}{50}{figure.caption.46}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Left: $\Phi $-sat-2 validation \acs {mIoU} over training at $10{,}000$ labelled patches, with the best epoch marked. Dotted line marks teacher's accuracy on Sentinel-2. Right: the residual accuracy difference between the two sensors for the distilled student, which crosses zero around epoch $55$ and oscillates. }}{52}{figure.caption.48}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Per-class test \acs {IoU} at $10{,}000$ labelled patches, ordered by class prevalence, with each class's share of labelled pixels beneath its label. Neither model leaves any class unlearned, which unweighted cross-entropy did for four of them (Section~\ref {sec:configuration}). }}{52}{figure.caption.49}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces Best validation \acs {mIoU} against labelled training budget. The two methods cross: distillation is worse at $1{,}000$ labelled patches and better at $10{,}000$. Both budgets share the same fixed validation split. }}{54}{figure.caption.52}%
